      summary: 'Deep networks were getting harder to train as they grew: adding more layers actually made the training error go up. The authors noticed that a deeper network could always just copy the shallower one and set the extra layers to do nothing, so the problem was optimization, not capacity. Their fix: each block learns a small residual correction F(x) and adds it to the input via a shortcut connection, so the network only has to learn what to change rather than the whole thing. This let them train a 152-layer network that is 8x deeper than VGG yet has lower computational cost. Their ensemble hit 3.57% top-5 error on ImageNet, winning ILSVRC 2015.',
