      oneIdea: 'Adam gives every parameter its own learning rate, automatically adjusted based on how large and how consistent that parameter\'s gradients have been over time.',
      summary: 'Training a neural network means nudging millions of numbers, one tiny step at a time, based on how wrong the model\'s last guess was. Standard methods apply the same step size to every number, which is wasteful. Adam keeps a running average of each parameter\'s gradient direction and a running average of how big those gradients have been, then scales each update by dividing direction by size. Parameters with consistently large gradients get smaller steps; parameters with small or noisy gradients get relatively larger ones. The paper shows Adam matches Adagrad on sparse IMDB data and beats SGD with momentum on MNIST, while a competing quasi-Newton method (SFO) runs 5-10x slower per iteration. The default settings (alpha = 0.001, beta1 = 0.9, beta2 = 0.999) worked across all tested models without tuning.',
