  PaperGroup('Scaling', [
    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-KAPLAN',
      short: 'Scaling Laws',
      year: '2020',
      authors: 'Jared Kaplan et al., OpenAI',
      oneIdea: 'Language model quality follows predictable power-law curves as you scale up model size, data, and compute. You can use simple equations to figure out the best way to spend your training budget.',
      summary: 'Before this paper, nobody had a clean formula for how much better a language model gets when you make it bigger, give it more data, or train it longer. Kaplan and colleagues trained hundreds of Transformer models ranging from 768 to 1.5 billion parameters on up to 22 billion tokens, and found that the loss drops as a smooth power law in each of those three factors, spanning more than seven orders of magnitude. The key practical result: if you double the parameters, the loss drops to about 0.95x (a 5% improvement), and if you make the model 8x bigger you only need about 5x more data to avoid overfitting. They also showed that when you have more compute, you should spend most of it on a bigger model (N grows as C^0.73) rather than training longer or using more data. Model shape (depth vs. width, number of heads) barely matters once you fix the total parameter count.',
      whyItMattered: 'It gave the field a mathematical compass for building bigger models. Instead of guessing whether a 10x bigger model would be worth it, you could calculate the expected loss and the data you would need. It also showed that bigger models are more sample-efficient, which changed how people think about the data-to-model-size ratio.',
      inYourTools: 'Model families come in sizes (for example 8B, 70B, 405B) because of the smooth "bigger is better" curves this paper measured: labs can predict how much better the next size will be before training it. When you choose a bigger model in Ollama or LM Studio and it is noticeably smarter, you are seeing these curves.',
      readThis: [
