    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-CHINCHILLA',
      short: 'Chinchilla',
      year: '2022',
      authors: 'Jordan Hoffmann et al., DeepMind',
      oneIdea: 'For a fixed compute budget, you should grow model size and training data at the same rate, not just make the model bigger and feed it the same amount of text.',
      summary: 'The field kept building bigger models but training them on roughly the same ~300 billion tokens, assuming more parameters meant better performance. Hoffmann and colleagues trained over 400 models (from 70M to 16B parameters, on 5B to 500B tokens) and used three separate methods to find the best size-and-data split for a given FLOP budget. All three methods agreed: scale parameters and tokens equally. They then built Chinchilla, a 70B-parameter model trained on 1.4 trillion tokens, using the same compute as Gopher (280B parameters, 300B tokens). Chinchilla beat Gopher, GPT-3, Jurassic-1, and MT-NLG 530B on nearly every benchmark, scoring 67.5% on MMLU, a 7-point gain over Gopher. The smaller size also meant far lower inference and fine-tuning costs.',
      whyItMattered: 'It showed that the \'bigger is better\' race was wasting compute: a smaller model trained much longer outperforms a larger model trained briefly. This shifted the industry toward training on trillions of tokens with modest parameter counts, making strong models cheaper to run on everyday hardware.',
      inYourTools: 'Every size choice in an open model family is shaped by this paper. Later models went even further than Chinchilla\'s "about 20 tokens per parameter": small models such as Llama 3 8B were trained on many trillions of tokens because a small, well-fed model is cheap to run on home hardware. That is why a 7-9B model in Ollama or LM Studio is far better than older models of the same size.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: The overlaid predictions from all three approaches plus Kaplan et al., showing that current models should be much smaller and trained much longer.',
        'Table 2: The exponents a and b for each approach, all hovering around 0.5 for both parameters and tokens, versus Kaplan\'s 0.73 and 0.27.',
