      year: '2022',
      authors: 'Long Ouyang et al., OpenAI',
      oneIdea: 'Instead of making a language model bigger, you can make it follow instructions better by training it on human preferences: first show it good examples, then reward it for outputs that humans rank above others.',
      summary: 'Large language models make up facts, generate toxic text, and often ignore what the user actually asked for. The OpenAI team fine-tuned GPT-3 in two steps: first supervised learning on human-written demonstrations, then reinforcement learning using a reward model trained on human rankings of model outputs. The 1.3B InstructGPT model was preferred over the 175B GPT-3 in head-to-head human evaluations, despite having 100x fewer parameters. The 175B InstructGPT won 85% of the time against 175B GPT-3. On closed-domain tasks it hallucinates at 21% versus 41% for GPT-3, and it generates about 25% fewer toxic outputs when prompted to be respectful.',
      whyItMattered: 'It proved that a smaller, aligned model beats a larger unaligned one, and that human preference data is a practical way to steer model behavior. This two-step recipe (supervised fine-tuning, then RLHF) became the standard method behind ChatGPT and most modern instruction-tuned models.',
      inYourTools: 'Almost every chat model you use, open or closed, went through a version of this recipe: supervised fine-tuning on good examples, then preference tuning (RLHF as in this paper, or later variants such as DPO). The "Instruct" or "Chat" in a model\'s name on Hugging Face means it went through this step; the "Base" version did not.',
