      oneIdea: 'Instead of paying humans to label every harmful AI response, you give the model a short list of written rules (a \'constitution\') and let it critique, revise, and judge its own outputs against those rules.',
      summary: 'Training AI to be harmless used to require tens of thousands of human labels, and the resulting models became evasive and unhelpful. The Anthropic team replaced human harmlessness labels with AI-generated ones, steered by 16 written principles. In a supervised phase, the model critiques and revises its own responses, then is fine-tuned on the revisions. In an RL phase, a separate model judges pairs of responses against the principles, and the assistant is trained with that AI feedback. The result: the RL-CAI model was significantly more harmless than human-feedback-trained models while staying helpful, and it was virtually never evasive.',
