    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-DPO',
      short: 'DPO',
      year: '2023',
      authors: 'Rafael Rafailov et al., Stanford University and CZ Biohub',
      oneIdea: 'The language model you are fine-tuning already is the reward model. You can skip the separate reward model and the reinforcement learning loop entirely, and just train the model with a simple classification loss on preference pairs.',
      summary: 'Aligning a language model with human preferences used to require a two-step RLHF pipeline: first train a reward model, then use reinforcement learning (PPO) to fine-tune the LM against that reward. This process was complex, unstable, and expensive. The authors showed that the optimal policy under the standard RLHF objective has a closed-form expression, so you can rewrite the loss as a function of the policy directly. DPO minimizes a binary cross-entropy loss on preference pairs, with no reward model, no RL loop, and no sampling from the policy during training. On summarization, DPO reached a win rate of approximately 61% against reference summaries at temperature 0.0, beating PPO\'s 57%. On single-turn dialogue, DPO was the only computationally efficient method that improved over the preferred completions, using models up to 6B parameters.',
      whyItMattered: 'DPO turned preference-based alignment into a single, stable training step that looks like ordinary supervised learning. It removed the need to train a separate reward model, run an RL loop, and sample from the policy during training, which cut compute costs and hyperparameter tuning. This lowered the barrier for labs and hobbyists to align models with human preferences.',
      inYourTools: 'Fine-tuning libraries such as Hugging Face TRL, Axolotl and Unsloth offer DPO as a ready-made training mode, and many open-weight chat models were preference-tuned with DPO or one of its many variants. If you ever fine-tune a model on "chosen vs rejected" answer pairs, this is the method you are using.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: Side-by-side comparison of the RLHF pipeline (reward model + RL) versus DPO (direct classification loss).',
        'Section 4, Eq. 5 and Eq. 7: The key reparameterization that turns the reward into a function of the policy, and the resulting DPO loss.',
        'Figure 2 (left): The reward-KL frontier showing DPO strictly dominates PPO on sentiment generation, even PPO with ground-truth rewards.',
      ],
      skipThis: 'The theoretical analysis in Section 5 (equivalence classes of reward functions, Plackett-Luce models, and the actor-critic instability argument) is dense and can be safely skipped on a first read.',
