      short: 'Chain-of-Thought',
      year: '2022',
      authors: 'Jason Wei et al., Google Research, Brain Team',
      oneIdea: 'If you show a large language model a few examples where it writes out its reasoning steps before giving an answer, it starts doing the same thing on new problems and gets much better at multi-step tasks.',
      summary: 'Large language models could get bigger and bigger, but they still stumbled on tasks that require more than one step of reasoning, like solving a math word problem. The authors at Google showed that simply including a few prompt examples where the model writes out its intermediate steps in plain English before stating the answer fixes this. PaLM 540B with just 8 such exemplars scored 57% on the GSM8K math benchmark, compared to 18% with standard prompting and 55% for the prior state-of-the-art finetuned model. The effect is an emergent property of scale: models below roughly 100B parameters actually perform worse when asked to reason step by step. The technique worked across arithmetic, commonsense, and symbolic reasoning tasks. No model was finetuned; the entire method is just a different way to write the prompt.',
      whyItMattered: 'It showed that how you ask can matter as much as model size: with a few worked examples, large models solved far more multi-step maths and logic problems. It led directly to training models to reason before answering.',
      inYourTools: 'Asking a model to "think step by step" is chain-of-thought prompting. Today\'s reasoning models (the "thinking" modes in chat apps, or open models that write a <think> section before answering) are trained to do this on their own, so the idea moved from the prompt into the training.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: Side-by-side comparison of standard prompting (direct answer) versus chain-of-thought prompting (reasoning steps then answer) on the same math problem.',
