      oneIdea: 'Before generating an answer, the model looks up relevant passages from an external text index and uses them as extra context, so it can answer questions it does not have memorized.',
      summary: 'Pre-trained language models store facts in their weights, but they cannot easily update that knowledge or show where an answer came from. The authors paired a BART generator (400M parameters) with a dense-vector retriever over 21 million Wikipedia passages and fine-tuned the two together end-to-end. On open-domain question answering, RAG-Sequence hit 44.5 exact match on Natural Questions, beating the 34.5 from a T5-11B model that relies only on its parameters. On Jeopardy question generation, human evaluators judged RAG more factual in 42.7% of 452 head-to-head comparisons versus 7.1% for BART. The team also showed that swapping the Wikipedia index from a 2016 dump to a 2018 dump updated the model\'s world knowledge without any retraining.',
      whyItMattered: 'It showed that a model can look things up instead of memorising everything: the index can be updated without retraining, and the passages it used can be shown as sources. RAG became one of the most common ways to build AI assistants on private data.',
      inYourTools: '"Chat with your documents" features work this way: Open WebUI\'s knowledge bases and LM Studio\'s document chat split your files into chunks, find the closest ones to your question with an embedding model and paste them into the prompt. Web search in chat apps is the same idea with the internet as the index.',
