    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-REACT',
      short: 'ReAct',
      year: '2022',
      authors: 'Shunyu Yao et al., Princeton University and Google Research',
      oneIdea: 'Let a language model alternate between thinking out loud and taking a real action (like searching a wiki page), so each step feeds the next and the model can check its own facts against the outside world.',
      summary: 'Large language models could reason step by step (chain-of-thought) or generate actions in an environment, but each approach alone had blind spots: pure reasoning hallucinated facts, and pure acting lost track of the plan. The authors prompted a frozen 540B-parameter model to write a short reasoning thought, then take an action such as searching Wikipedia, then read the result, then think again, using only one to six human-written examples. On the ALFWorld text game, this one-to-two-example prompt beat imitation and reinforcement learning agents trained on thousands of episodes by 34 percentage points in success rate. On WebShop, the same approach improved success rate by 10 points over the best prior method. On fact verification, ReAct scored 60.9% versus 56.3% for chain-of-thought, because the model could verify claims against Wikipedia instead of relying on memory alone.',
      whyItMattered: 'It showed that you do not need to train a model to use tools; a simple prompt that interleaves thinking and acting already produces grounded, interpretable behavior. It became the template for how modern LLM agents work: think, call a tool, read the result, think again. The reasoning traces make it easy for a human to see why the model did what it did and to correct it mid-task.',
      inYourTools: 'Agents work in a think → act → observe loop like this: when an assistant searches the web, runs code or calls a tool (for example through MCP in Claude Code or Open WebUI), it decides an action, reads the result, and thinks again. Agent frameworks such as LangChain named their basic agent after this paper.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: Side-by-side comparison of Standard, CoT, Act-only, and ReAct trajectories on a HotpotQA question and an ALFWorld game, showing exactly how thoughts and actions interleave.',
        'Table 1: PaLM-540B prompting results on HotpotQA and Fever, where you can see ReAct beat CoT on Fever (60.9 vs 56.3) and the combined ReAct+CoT-SC methods scored highest overall.',
        'Table 2: Breakdown of success and failure modes for ReAct and CoT, showing that CoT hallucinates in 56% of its failures while ReAct\'s main failure is reasoning error at 47%.',
