      oneIdea: 'A language model can teach itself to use external tools (a calculator, a search engine, a translator) without any human-labeled data, by keeping only the tool calls that actually help it predict the next words.',
      summary: 'Large language models are strong at language but weak at arithmetic, factual lookup, and knowing the current date. The authors let a 6.7B-parameter GPT-J model propose API calls to five tools on a large text corpus, then kept only the calls that reduced the model\'s loss on the tokens that follow. They finetuned the model on those filtered calls. The result: Toolformer beat GPT-3 (175B) on math benchmarks (40.4 vs 14.0 on ASDiv) and fact-filling tasks (53.5 vs 39.8 on T-REx), while its language modeling perplexity stayed the same. The model used the calculator for 97.9% of math examples and the Q&A tool for 98.1% of fact examples, deciding on its own when to call each tool.',
      whyItMattered: 'It showed that a model can learn when a tool call helps by checking whether the result makes the following text easier to predict, with only a few examples per tool. It was an early step toward the function-calling models that agents rely on.',
      inYourTools: 'Tool calling ("function calling") is now built into most models and engines: vLLM, llama.cpp\'s server and LM Studio expose it through their OpenAI-compatible APIs, and front-ends use it for web search, calculators or MCP tools. Today it is mostly taught with fine-tuning on tool-use examples rather than Toolformer\'s self-supervised trick.',
