    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-LORA',
      short: 'LoRA',
      year: '2021',
      authors: 'Edward Hu et al., Microsoft',
      oneIdea: 'Freeze the big model and only train two tiny matrices whose product captures the small change a task needs. At inference time the model runs exactly as before, with no extra latency.',
      summary: 'Full fine-tuning of a 175-billion-parameter model like GPT-3 means storing and updating every weight, which is prohibitively expensive in memory and storage. LoRA freezes all original weights and instead trains two small matrices (A and B) whose product approximates the change each layer requires. The authors show that even a rank as small as 1 or 2 captures enough of the needed change, even when the full dimension is 12,288. On GPT-3 175B, LoRA cuts trainable parameters by 10,000 times and GPU memory by 3 times. It matches or beats full fine-tuning on quality while adding zero extra inference latency.',
      whyItMattered: 'Before LoRA, adapting a large model to a new task meant either retraining the entire model or adding layers that slowed down every inference call. LoRA made it practical to fine-tune big models on consumer hardware and to swap between many task-specific adapters cheaply. It became the default approach for efficient fine-tuning in the open-source LLM ecosystem.',
      inYourTools: 'Most fine-tunes you find on Hugging Face were trained with LoRA or QLoRA (for example with Unsloth, Axolotl or Hugging Face PEFT). llama.cpp can load a LoRA adapter on top of a base model (--lora), Ollama supports it with the ADAPTER line in a Modelfile, and vLLM can serve many LoRA adapters on one base model at the same time.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: The one-page diagram showing how W0 + BA replaces the original weight, with A and B being the only trainable parts.',
        'Table 4: GPT-3 175B results where LoRA with 4.7M trainable parameters matches or beats full fine-tuning on WikiSQL and MNLI.',
