  PaperGroup('Running models fast', [
    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-FLASHATTN',
      short: 'FlashAttention',
      year: '2022',
      authors: 'Tri Dao et al., Stanford University and University at Buffalo',
      oneIdea: 'Attention can be made faster and use less memory by being IO-aware: carefully managing which data lives in the GPU\'s tiny fast on-chip memory versus its large slow main memory, rather than just cutting arithmetic.',
      summary: 'Standard attention writes the full N-by-N score matrix to slow GPU memory (HBM), which makes it both slow and memory-hungry for long sequences. FlashAttention splits Q, K, and V into small blocks, loads them into fast on-chip SRAM, and computes attention block by block without ever materializing the full N-by-N matrix in HBM. It also avoids storing that matrix for the backward pass by recomputing it on-chip from a few saved statistics. The result is up to 3x end-to-end speedup on GPT-2 training and memory that scales linearly (not quadratically) with sequence length. The same idea extends to block-sparse attention, which is 2-4x faster than even FlashAttention when the attention pattern is sparse.',
      whyItMattered: 'Before FlashAttention, long context was limited by GPU memory traffic, not just arithmetic. By never writing the full attention matrix to slow memory, it made training and running with much longer sequences practical, and its ideas became the default in major frameworks.',
      inYourTools: 'vLLM and Hugging Face Transformers use FlashAttention kernels on supported NVIDIA GPUs. llama.cpp has its own implementation of the same idea, switched on with the -fa (--flash-attn) option; it saves memory and usually speeds things up at long context, which is why many guides tell you to turn it on.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: The left panel shows the GPU memory hierarchy (SRAM vs. HBM) and how tiling avoids writing the N-by-N matrix to HBM; the right panel shows the 7.6x speedup on attention alone.',
        'Section 3.2: The IO complexity analysis proving FlashAttention needs far fewer HBM accesses than standard attention, plus the lower-bound result.',
        'Table 2: Side-by-side training times for GPT-2 small and medium with HuggingFace, Megatron-LM, and FlashAttention, showing up to 3x speedup.',
      ],
      skipThis: 'The formal IO-complexity proofs in Section 3.2 and Appendix C can be safely skipped; the core intuition (fewer memory copies means faster) is what matters for a practitioner.',
      terms: [
