    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-VLLM',
      short: 'vLLM',
      year: '2023',
      authors: 'Woosuk Kwon et al., UC Berkeley / Stanford / UC San Diego',
      oneIdea: 'The key-value cache of a running LLM can be split into small fixed-size blocks that do not need to sit next to each other in memory, just like an operating system pages virtual memory. This eliminates most of the wasted GPU space and lets you batch far more requests at once.',
      summary: 'When serving an LLM, the key-value cache for each request grows and shrinks as tokens are generated. Existing systems pre-allocate a contiguous chunk of memory for the maximum possible sequence length, wasting most of it: only 20.4% to 38.2% of that space actually stores token states. PagedAttention splits the KV cache into fixed-size blocks that can live anywhere in GPU memory, and vLLM manages those blocks with a block table, allocating them on demand. This near-zero waste means many more requests fit in memory at the same time. In their benchmarks, vLLM delivers 2 to 4 times higher throughput than FasterTransformer and Orca at the same latency, and the gap widens with longer sequences and larger models.',
      whyItMattered: 'Before vLLM, GPU memory was the bottleneck for serving LLMs: you could only batch a handful of requests before running out of space for the KV cache. By reclaiming the wasted memory, vLLM made it practical to serve many concurrent users on a single GPU, cutting the cost per request. It became the default inference engine for a large share of open-source LLM deployments.',
      inYourTools: 'vLLM is a widely used open-source serving engine with an OpenAI-compatible API; front-ends such as Open WebUI can connect to it like any other API. It shines when many requests run at once (several users, agents, batch jobs) on NVIDIA or AMD GPUs; for one person on one consumer GPU, llama.cpp-based tools are often simpler.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: Shows how GPU memory is split between model weights (~65%) and KV cache (~30%) on an A100, and how vLLM smooths the rapid KV cache growth curve that limits batch size.',
        'Figure 2: Bar chart showing that existing serving systems waste 60-80% of KV cache memory, with only 20.4% to 38.2% storing actual token states.',
        'Section 4.1: The PagedAttention algorithm explained with the block-wise attention formula and the \'Four score and seven\' example of non-contiguous blocks.',
      ],
      skipThis: 'The distributed execution details in Section 4.6 and the kernel-level optimization tricks in Section 5.1 are implementation specifics that will not change how you use the tool.',
