    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-GPTQ',
      short: 'GPTQ',
      year: '2022',
      authors: 'Elias Frantar et al., IST Austria & ETH Zurich',
      oneIdea: 'You can shrink a huge language model to 3 or 4 bits per weight in one pass, without retraining, by using second-order math to nudge the remaining weights after every rounding step.',
      summary: 'Large models like GPT-3 175B need 326 GB of memory in 16-bit format, which does not fit on a single GPU. GPTQ quantizes the model layer by layer, one weight at a time, and after each rounding it adjusts all remaining unquantized weights using second-order (Hessian) information to compensate for the error. This one-shot process takes only about 4 GPU hours for a 175B model and uses just 128 short text snippets for calibration. At 4-bit, GPTQ loses only 0.03 perplexity on OPT-175B, while simple rounding (RTN) drops 2.2 points and at 3-bit RTN collapses entirely. The 3-bit model fits in a single 80 GB A100 and runs 3.25x faster than the 5-GPU FP16 baseline.',
      whyItMattered: 'For the first time, a 175-billion-parameter model could be compressed to 3-4 bits in a few hours on one GPU without retraining, and still run generation on a single GPU. It made low-bit open models practical and started the wave of quantized releases that home users rely on.',
      inYourTools: 'On Hugging Face, models with "GPTQ" in the name are 3-8-bit versions made with this method, mostly run on NVIDIA GPUs with vLLM, SGLang, Transformers or ExLlama. GGUF files for llama.cpp, Ollama and LM Studio use llama.cpp\'s own quant types instead, but they rely on the same discovery: careful rounding keeps 4-bit models close to full quality.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: Side-by-side perplexity curves for OPT at 4-bit and BLOOM at 3-bit, showing where RTN collapses and GPTQ holds steady.',
        'Table 3: Full OPT perplexity results across all model sizes at 4-bit and 3-bit; the 175B row makes the accuracy gap concrete.',
        'Section 4, Steps 1-3: The three algorithmic insights (fixed order, lazy batch updates, Cholesky reformulation) that turn a cubic-time method into one that finishes in hours.',
      ],
      skipThis: 'The vision-model benchmarks in Table 1 and the detailed OBQ background in Section 3 are interesting for ML researchers but not needed to understand what GPTQ does for you.',
      terms: [
        ('quantization', 'Reducing each weight from 16 bits to 3 or 4 bits to save memory.'),
        ('post-training', 'Compressing a model after it is already trained, without retraining it.'),
        ('perplexity', 'A lower-is-better score; higher means the model is more surprised by text.'),
        ('Hessian', 'A matrix describing how sensitive the model output is to each weight.'),
        ('one-shot', 'Done in a single pass over a small data sample, no iterative retraining.'),
      ],
      check: [
