    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-AWQ',
      short: 'AWQ',
      year: '2023',
      authors: 'Ji Lin et al., MIT / NVIDIA / Shanghai Jiao Tong University / Tsinghua University / MIT-IBM Watson AI Lab / UMass Amherst',
      oneIdea: 'Not all weights in an LLM are equally important. By looking at activation magnitudes (not weight values) to find the tiny fraction of channels that matter, and scaling those channels up before quantizing, you protect them from quantization error while keeping every weight at the same low bit width.',
      summary: 'Running large models on a laptop or phone is hard because the weights take up too much memory. Naive 4-bit quantization destroys accuracy because it treats every weight the same. AWQ notices that about 1% of weight channels carry most of the signal, identified by how large their activations are. Instead of keeping those channels in FP16 (which confuses hardware), it scales them up before quantizing, which mathematically shrinks their relative rounding error. On OPT-6.7B at INT3, this brought perplexity from 23.54 down to 11.92. Their inference engine TinyChat turns the 4-bit savings into a measured 3.2-3.3x speedup over HuggingFace FP16 on desktop and mobile GPUs.',
      whyItMattered: 'It showed that a few percent of weight channels, found by looking at activations, carry most of the accuracy, and that protecting them keeps 4-bit models close to full quality without any retraining. Because it needs only a small calibration set and no backpropagation, it became one of the standard ways to publish 4-bit models for GPU servers.',
      inYourTools: 'On Hugging Face you will find many models with "AWQ" in the name: 4-bit versions made with this method, loaded by vLLM, SGLang or Hugging Face Transformers on NVIDIA GPUs. GGUF files for llama.cpp, Ollama and LM Studio use llama.cpp\'s own quant types instead, but the same lesson (protect the weights that matter most, found with sample text) lives on in llama.cpp\'s importance matrix (imatrix).',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 2: the three-panel comparison showing RTN (PPL 43.2), keeping 1% in FP16 (PPL 13.0), and AWQ scaling (PPL 13.0) on OPT-6.7B INT3.',
        'Table 2: the scaling-factor sweep on OPT-6.7B showing perplexity dropping from 23.54 at s=1 to 11.92 at s=2, and why larger s hurts non-salient channels.',
        'Section 3.2: the short derivation of why multiplying a salient weight by s>1 reduces its relative quantization error (Equation 2-3).',
      ],
      skipThis: 'The TinyChat system section (Section 4) on kernel fusion, SIMD-aware weight packing, and roofline analysis is engineering detail you do not need to understand the quantization idea itself.',
      terms: [
        ('Quantization', 'Reducing weight precision from 16-bit floats to 4-bit integers to save memory.'),
        ('Salient weights', 'The small fraction of channels that carry the most important information.'),
        ('Activation', 'The values a neuron outputs; larger values mean more important features.'),
        ('Per-channel scaling', 'Multiplying each weight channel by a different factor before quantizing.'),
        ('Calibration set', 'A small batch of inputs used to measure activation statistics, not to train.'),
      ],
      check: [
        QuizQuestion(
          'What tells AWQ which weight channels are most important?',
          [
            'The weight values themselves',
            'The activation magnitudes',
            'Random selection',
            'The L2-norm of the weights',
          ],
          1,
          'The paper explicitly states you should refer to the activation distribution, not the weight distribution, to identify salient channels.',
        ),
        QuizQuestion(
          'Why does AWQ scale up salient channels instead of simply keeping them in FP16?',
          [
            'To increase model accuracy',
            'To reduce calibration time',
            'To make the model larger',
            'Because mixed-precision is hardware-inefficient',
          ],
          3,
          'Keeping a few channels in FP16 while the rest are INT4 creates a mixed-precision format that GPUs and CPUs handle poorly.',
        ),
        QuizQuestion(
          'What does AWQ explicitly NOT rely on?',
          ['Backpropagation', 'A calibration set', 'Activation statistics', 'A GPU'],
          0,
          'AWQ only measures average activation magnitudes from a small calibration set; it needs no gradient updates or weight reconstruction.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-KIVI',
      short: 'KIVI',
      year: '2024',
      authors: 'Zirui Liu et al., Rice University, Texas A&M University, Stevens Institute of Technology, Carnegie Mellon University',
      oneIdea: 'Key cache has a few fixed channels with huge values, so it should be quantized per-channel; value cache has no such pattern, so it should be quantized per-token. This asymmetric 2-bit scheme works without any tuning.',
      summary: 'When you run many requests or long conversations, the KV cache (the stored attention keys and values) balloons in size and slows everything down. The authors studied the actual numbers inside KV cache and found that key cache has a handful of channels with very large magnitudes, while value cache looks uniform. They built KIVI, which quantizes keys per-channel and values per-token at 2 bits, keeping a small full-precision window for the most recent tokens. On Llama-2-7B this cuts peak memory by 2.6x and enables up to 4x larger batch sizes, giving 2.35x to 3.47x throughput on real workloads. The accuracy drop is around 2% on hard tasks like GSM8K.',
      whyItMattered: 'It showed that keys and values behave differently (keys have a few huge outlier channels, values do not) and that treating them differently lets the cache go down to 2 bits without retraining. That made the KV cache a serious target for compression, which matters more as contexts grow.',
      inYourTools: 'llama.cpp can store the KV cache in 8 or 4 bits (-ctk / -ctv, e.g. q8_0), and Ollama and LM Studio expose the same option; it saves a lot of memory at long context. Those are simpler schemes than KIVI\'s 2-bit one, but the goal is the same: the cache, not just the weights, can be squeezed.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 2: Side-by-side heatmaps showing key cache has a few bright outlier channels while value cache is uniform.',
        'Table 1: The 2-bit comparison on Llama-2-13B showing that K-per-channel + V-per-token keeps accuracy near 16-bit while all other 2-bit configs collapse.',
        'Section 3.2: The explanation of why per-token quantization of value cache gives 15x smaller error than per-channel, because attention is 84.3% sparse.',
      ],
      skipThis: 'The CUDA/Triton kernel details and the ablation on group size and residual length are implementation specifics you can skip unless you are writing your own quantization code.',
      terms: [
        ('KV cache', 'Stored keys and values from previous tokens, avoiding attention recomputation.'),
        ('Per-channel quantization', 'Quantizing all values within one channel together, sharing one scale.'),
        ('Per-token quantization', 'Quantizing all values within one token together, sharing one scale.'),
        ('Attention sparsity', 'Most attention scores are near zero; only a few tokens dominate the output.'),
        ('Sliding window', 'A small buffer of recent tokens kept at full precision for accuracy.'),
      ],
      check: [
        QuizQuestion(
          'Why does key cache use per-channel quantization in KIVI?',
          [
            'Because it has a few fixed channels with very large values (outliers)',
            'Because per-channel is always more accurate than per-token',
            'Because the model was trained with per-channel quantization',
            'Because value cache requires it',
          ],
          0,
          'Figure 2 and Section 3.2 show that key cache has persistent outlier channels, so per-channel quantization confines the error to those channels.',
        ),
        QuizQuestion(
          'Why does value cache use per-token quantization in KIVI?',
          [
            'Because value cache has fixed outlier channels',
            'Because per-token is the only option for streaming inference',
            'Because attention is sparse, so quantizing one token does not corrupt the output of important tokens',
            'Because value magnitudes are always small',
          ],
          2,
          'Section 3.2 explains that attention sparsity (84.3%) means the output depends on only a few tokens, so per-token error confinement keeps the relative error 15x smaller.',
        ),
        QuizQuestion(
          'What role does the full-precision sliding window play in KIVI?',
          [
            'It stores the entire prompt at full precision',
            'It keeps the most recent tokens at full precision, which is crucial for hard tasks like mathematical reasoning',
            'It replaces the quantized cache after generation is complete',
            'It reduces the group size used for quantization',
          ],
          1,
          'Section 3.3 and 4.2.1 show that the full-precision window (residual length R) is what keeps GSM8K accuracy within about 2% of the 16-bit baseline.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-SPECDEC',
      short: 'Speculative Decoding',
      year: '2022',
      authors: 'Yaniv Leviathan et al., Google Research',
      oneIdea: 'A small model drafts a few tokens, and the big model checks all of them in a single parallel pass. The output is mathematically identical to what the big model would produce on its own.',
      summary: 'Generating text from a large model is slow because every token requires a separate serial run. The authors use a much smaller model to draft several tokens at once, then run the large model in parallel to verify all the guesses in one step. It accepts the tokens that fit and samples a replacement from an adjusted distribution for the first one that does not. On T5-XXL (11B parameters), they measured speedups of 2.6x to 3.4x over the standard T5X implementation, with no change to the output distribution. The method needs no retraining, no architecture changes, and no new model training.',
      whyItMattered: 'It turned a serial bottleneck into a parallel one, giving large models a major latency cut without touching the model or its outputs. It became a standard technique in production LLM serving, where memory bandwidth is the real bottleneck and spare compute is available.',
      inYourTools: 'llama.cpp supports it with a small draft model (-md / --model-draft), LM Studio has a speculative decoding setting, and vLLM and SGLang offer it for serving. Pairing a big model with a tiny one from the same family (same tokenizer) can make replies noticeably faster with exactly the same output.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: a visual walkthrough of one iteration, showing green accepted tokens, red rejected tokens, and blue corrections',
        'Table 2: the measured speedup numbers for T5-XXL with T5-small, T5-base, and T5-large as draft models',
        'Section 2.3: the speculative sampling rule that guarantees the output distribution stays identical',
      ],
      skipThis: 'The divergence definitions and theorems in Section 3 (DLK, Theorem 3.5, Theorem 3.11) are dense math that a hobbyist can safely skip.',
      terms: [
        ('autoregressive model', 'A model that writes one token at a time, each depending on everything before it.'),
        (
          'speculative execution',
          'Doing a computation in parallel while simultaneously checking whether it was actually needed.',
        ),
        ('acceptance rate (alpha)', 'How often the small model\'s guesses match what the big model would have chosen.'),
        ('output distribution', 'The probability pattern of which tokens the model can produce; it stays unchanged.'),
        (
          'KV cache',
          'Stored attention keys and values that let the model remember prior tokens without re-reading them.',
        ),
      ],
      check: [
        QuizQuestion(
          'What is the role of the smaller draft model in speculative decoding?',
          [
            'It replaces the large model entirely for easy tokens',
            'It proposes several tokens that the large model then verifies in parallel',
            'It fine-tunes the large model\'s weights before generation',
            'It compresses the large model into fewer parameters',
          ],
          1,
          'The draft model autoregressively samples gamma guesses, and the target model evaluates all of them in a single parallel run.',
        ),
        QuizQuestion(
          'What happens when the large model rejects a drafted token?',
          [
            'The entire generation is discarded and restarted from the beginning',
            'The large model samples a replacement token from an adjusted distribution',
            'The small model is asked to try a different token',
            'The token is kept but its probability is reduced',
          ],
          1,
          'The algorithm samples from p\'(x) = norm(max(0, p(x) - q(x))) to produce the corrected token.',
        ),
        QuizQuestion(
          'What is guaranteed about the output of speculative decoding?',
          [
            'It is always shorter than standard decoding',
            'It has a higher probability of producing common words',
            'It has the exact same distribution as the large model alone',
            'It is always grammatically correct',
          ],
          2,
          'The paper proves that for any choice of draft model, the output distribution is mathematically identical to the target model\'s.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-MEDUSA',
      short: 'Medusa',
      year: '2024',
      authors: 'Tianle Cai et al., Princeton University / Together AI / University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign / Carnegie Mellon University / University of Connecticut',
      oneIdea: 'Instead of training a separate small model to guess ahead, you bolt a few tiny extra heads onto the big model itself. Each head predicts a different future token in parallel, and the model checks all of them at once.',
      summary: 'LLMs generate text one token at a time, and every single token requires shuffling all model parameters from high-bandwidth memory to the accelerator. That makes inference slow. The authors add a handful of small extra decoding heads on top of an existing model; each head predicts a different upcoming token. A tree-shaped attention mask lets the model verify all candidate continuations in one forward pass. MEDUSA-1 (frozen backbone) gives a 2.18x speedup on Vicuna-7B, and MEDUSA-2 (joint training) pushes that to 2.83x. No separate draft model is needed, so the approach slots cleanly into any existing serving stack.',
      whyItMattered: 'It removed the need to find, train and serve a separate draft model: a few small heads on the big model do the guessing. That made "guess ahead, check fast" much easier to add, and built-in guessing heads became common in newer models.',
      inYourTools: 'Serving engines such as vLLM, SGLang and TensorRT-LLM support speculative decoding, including Medusa- and EAGLE-style heads that live inside the model. The same idea is behind the multi-token prediction (MTP) heads of newer models such as DeepSeek-V3, which engines use to speed up generation.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: Shows the MEDUSA heads sitting on top of the model and how candidates are assembled and verified in a single step.',
        'Table 3: Breaks down the speedup contribution of each piece: heads alone (~1.5x), adding tree attention (~1.9x), optimized tree (~2.2x), and Medusa-2 training (~2.8x).',
        'Section 2.3.1: Explains the typical acceptance scheme that replaces rejection sampling and lets higher temperatures actually help speed.',
      ],
      skipThis: 'The Impact Statement on societal, environmental, and ethical considerations adds no technical insight and can be skipped safely.',
      terms: [
        ('Auto-regressive decoding', 'Generating text one token at a time, each one depending on all previous ones.'),
        ('Speculative decoding', 'Guessing several future tokens ahead so the big model can verify them in one step.'),
        (
          'Tree attention',
          'An attention mask that lets multiple candidate continuations be checked at once in a single pass.',
        ),
        ('Draft model', 'A smaller model that guesses what the big model would say next.'),
        (
          'Rejection sampling',
          'Accepting or rejecting a candidate based on whether it matches the original model\'s probabilities.',
        ),
      ],
      check: [
        QuizQuestion(
          'What does MEDUSA add to an existing LLM to speed up inference?',
