    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-MOEOFFLOAD',
      short: 'MoE Offloading',
      year: '2023',
      authors: 'Artyom Eliseev et al., Moscow Institute of Physics and Technology / Yandex',
      oneIdea: 'MoE models are too big to fit in a consumer GPU, but because only a few experts are active per token, you can keep the recently used ones cached and guess the next ones ahead of time, so the GPU never has to wait for a full layer load.',
      summary: 'Mixtral-8x7B has 46.7B parameters, 96.6% of which are experts, and it does not fit in 12-16 GB of VRAM. Naive offloading loads the entire expert layer for every token, which is slow because interactive generation is one token at a time. The authors noticed two things: the same experts get reused across adjacent tokens, and the hidden states from one layer already predict which experts the next layer will need. They built a system that keeps a small LRU cache of recently used experts in VRAM and speculatively pre-loads the most likely next experts while the current layer is still computing. Combined with mixed quantization (4-bit for attention, 2-3 bit for experts), this runs Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct at 2-3 tokens per second on a desktop RTX 3060 or a free-tier Colab T4.',
      whyItMattered: 'It showed that Mixtral-class MoE models could run interactively on consumer GPUs and free cloud notebooks by caching recently used experts and loading likely next experts early. Offloading experts to system RAM is now a standard way to run big MoE models at home.',
      inYourTools: 'llama.cpp can keep MoE expert weights in system RAM while the rest runs on the GPU (--cpu-moe, --n-cpu-moe), and its newer --moe-cache-mib option keeps a cache of experts in VRAM, the same trick this paper used. That is how people run very large MoE models on one gaming GPU plus lots of RAM.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: the color-coded grid showing which experts fire for each token across layers 0 and 15, with gray squares marking the LRU cache (k=2).',
        'Section 3.2: the explanation of why you cannot pre-fetch the next layer\'s experts in a MoE model, and how applying the next layer\'s gating function to the current hidden states gives a reliable guess.',
        'Table 2: the side-by-side tokens-per-second numbers for the full algorithm versus removing pre-loading, removing the cache, and naive offloading across four GPUs.',
      ],
      skipThis: 'The full quantization sweep in Section 4.2 and Table 1 (every combination of 2/3/4-bit for attention and experts) is interesting for a quantization researcher but not essential for understanding the offloading idea.',
      terms: [
        ('Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)', 'A model where only a small subset of \'expert\' layers runs for each token.'),
        ('Offloading', 'Storing model weights in cheap RAM and loading them to the GPU just-in-time.'),
        ('LRU cache', 'Keep the most recently used items; evict the least recently used when full.'),
        ('Gating function', 'A small network that picks which experts should process the current token.'),
        (
          'Mixed quantization',
          'Compress different parts of a model to different bit-widths, e.g. 4-bit attention, 2-bit experts.',
        ),
      ],
      check: [
        QuizQuestion(
          'Why can\'t a MoE model use the same simple \'pre-load the next layer\' trick that works for dense models?',
          [
            'The experts are too large to fit in PCIe transfer buffers',
            'The model does not know which experts to load until the current layer finishes computing',
            'The gating function requires a separate GPU pass',
            'The experts are stored in a random order in RAM',
          ],
          1,
          'MoE layers choose their active experts based on the hidden states they receive, so the system cannot fetch the right experts until the previous layer has produced those states.',
        ),
        QuizQuestion(
          'What does the LRU expert cache actually do during token generation?',
          [
            'It re-trains experts that are used frequently',
            'It keeps the last k active experts in VRAM so they are instantly available if reused on the next token',
            'It compresses unused experts to 1-bit to save space',
            'It moves all experts from RAM to GPU in a fixed round-robin order',
          ],
          1,
          'The cache holds the k most recently used experts per layer in GPU memory, so if the same expert is selected again, no RAM-to-GPU transfer is needed.',
        ),
        QuizQuestion(
          'How does speculative expert loading work without changing the model\'s output?',
          [
            'It applies the next layer\'s gating function to the current layer\'s hidden states and loads the top-1 or top-2 predicted experts in the background',
            'It duplicates every expert into VRAM so no loading is ever needed',
            'It waits for the user to type the next character and pre-loads accordingly',
            'It uses a separate small model to predict which experts will be needed',
          ],
          0,
          'Because transformer layers are residual, the current hidden states are a good proxy for the next layer\'s input, so the gating function can be run early to guess the right experts; if the guess is wrong, the correct ones are loaded later.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-YARN',
      short: 'YaRN',
      year: '2023',
      authors: 'Bowen Peng et al., Nous Research, EleutherAI, University of Geneva',
      oneIdea: 'You can make a language model read much longer texts by selectively tweaking how it encodes position, without retraining it from scratch. It takes far less data and compute than earlier methods.',
      summary: 'LLMs trained with Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE) break down on texts longer than their training context. The authors show that stretching every RoPE frequency equally, as Position Interpolation does, destroys the high-frequency positional signals the model still needs. YaRN fixes this by scaling only the low-frequency dimensions and leaving high-frequency ones untouched, then adding a small temperature tweak to the attention logits. The result is a model that reads 128k tokens after just 400 fine-tuning steps on roughly 0.1% of the original pre-training data, using 10x fewer tokens and 2.5x fewer steps than prior methods. The s=32 model, trained only on 64k-token chunks, successfully extrapolates to 128k context.',
      whyItMattered: 'Before YaRN, extending a model\'s context window meant expensive retraining or accepting degraded quality, which made long-context models impractical for most labs. YaRN turned context extension into a cheap, few-hundred-step fine-tune, and its compatibility with Flash Attention 2 meant it could be adopted immediately by the open-source community.',
      inYourTools: 'Qwen3\'s model cards tell you to switch on YaRN to go from 32,768 to 131,072 tokens of context; in llama.cpp that is the --rope-scaling yarn option. Many long-context versions of open models were made with YaRN.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: The diagram showing how PI, NTK-aware, NTK-by-parts, and YaRN relate to each other.',
        'Section 3.2: The NTK-by-parts interpolation, where the paper explains why high-frequency dimensions should not be stretched and introduces the ramp function.',
        'Table 1: Perplexity scores showing the s=32 model (trained on 64k data) successfully handles 128k context.',
      ],
      skipThis: 'The complex-number derivations in Section 2.1 and the NTK-aware base-change algebra in Appendix A.2 are for the curious, not the essential.',
      terms: [
        (
          'RoPE (Rotary Position Embedding)',
          'A way to mark each token\'s position by rotating vectors at different speeds.',
        ),
        ('Context window', 'The maximum number of tokens a model can read at once.'),
        ('Interpolation', 'Rescaling position signals so the model can read longer texts.'),
        ('Scale factor', 'How many times longer the new context is versus the original.'),
        ('Perplexity', 'A score where lower means the model is less surprised by the text.'),
      ],
      check: [
        QuizQuestion(
          'What is the main problem YaRN solves?',
          [
            'Making models generate faster tokens',
