      oneIdea: 'Instead of running every parameter for every token, the model has 8 small "expert" blocks per layer and only activates 2 of them, so it gets big-model knowledge while doing a fraction of the compute.',
      summary: 'Dense models use all their weights for every single token, which is slow and expensive. Mistral AI built Mixtral 8x7B by replacing the feedforward block in each of its 32 layers with 8 separate expert blocks; a small router network picks 2 of those 8 for each token. The model has 47B total parameters but only 13B are active per token. On standard benchmarks it matches or beats Llama 2 70B, scoring 70.6% on MMLU and 74.4% on GSM8K versus 69.9% and 69.6% for the 70B model. The Instruct version scored 8.30 on MT-Bench, outperforming GPT-3.5 Turbo and Claude-2.1 in human evaluations.',
      whyItMattered: 'It proved that an open-weight model could deliver near-70B quality while only computing 13B worth of work per token, making strong models practical on consumer hardware. The Apache 2.0 release and vLLM integration lowered the barrier for anyone wanting to self-host a capable model.',
