    Paper(
      sourceId: 'SRC-TTC',
      short: 'Compute-Optimal Scaling',
      year: '2024',
      authors: 'Charlie Snell et al., UC Berkeley / Google DeepMind',
      oneIdea: 'Instead of spending all your extra compute the same way on every question, you should pick the right strategy per prompt: easy questions get iterative self-revision, hard questions get parallel search. This adaptive allocation beats a fixed best-of-N approach by using up to 4x less compute.',
      summary: 'The problem: when you give a model more compute at test time, how do you spend it most effectively? The authors fine-tuned PaLM 2-S* on the MATH benchmark (12k train, 500 test questions) to do two things: iteratively revise its own answers, and verify solution steps with a process reward model. They found that the best strategy depends on question difficulty: easy questions benefit from sequential revision, hard questions benefit from parallel search. Their compute-optimal strategy, which picks the right approach per prompt, outperforms a fixed best-of-N baseline using 4x less compute. In a FLOPs-matched comparison, a smaller model with test-time compute can outperform a 14x larger model on easy and medium questions, though on the hardest problems pretraining still wins.',
      whyItMattered: 'It reframed the scaling question: you do not always need a bigger model. Spending FLOPs at inference time, adaptively per prompt, can substitute for pretraining on many real-world tasks. This pointed toward a future where models are smaller but think longer, rather than endlessly larger.',
      inYourTools: 'Reasoning models that "think" longer before answering are spending test-time compute. You can also do the simplest version yourself: generate several answers and pick the best one, or let the model check and revise its own answer.',
      readThis: [
        'Figure 1: The two-panel summary showing compute-optimal scaling beating best-of-N with 4x less compute, and the FLOPs-matched comparison against a 14x larger model.',
        'Section 3.2: How they estimate question difficulty by binning the model\'s pass@1 rate from 2048 samples into five quantiles, and why model-predicted difficulty works in practice.',
        'Figure 9: The tradeoff curves showing when test-time compute beats pretraining and when it does not, across difficulty levels and inference-to-pretraining token ratios.',
